{ Welcome to Chapter 4 of our White repertoire — mark this entry as Informational on Chessable.<br><br>Against the King's Indian Defense, classical lines like 5. Nf3 and 6. Be2 allow Black comfortable ...e5...Nh5, and ...f5 counterplay, while Petrosian's 7. d5 often stalls against 7... a5. Instead, we adopt Anatoly Karpov's signature E71 Makagonov System: 5. h3! followed by 6. Be3! — shutting down ...Bg4 and ...Ng4 ideas before locking or clamping the center on our own terms. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. h3 $1 { 5. h3! looks modest, yet it is the positional spine of the whole system. It keeps Black's c8-bishop off g4 forever, guards g4 for a later pawn storm, and scores 59.8% in Masters and 53.1% in 2000-2500 online play. } { [%csl Gh3,Yg4] [%cal Bc1e3,Gg2g4] } 5... O-O 6. Be3 $1 { 6. Be3 completes our central battery behind d4 without committing the g1-knight yet. Keeping Nf3 in reserve lets us answer 6... e5 with 7. d5 and immediate g4 plus Nge2-g3, while still allowing Nf3 against 6... c5 or 6... Nbd7. } { [%csl Ye3,Gd4] [%cal Be3d4,Ge4d5] } 6... e5 { 6... e5 is Black's #1 test at roughly 41-43% frequency, immediately challenging our center. Click Black's four other main move-6 systems below to preview our unified roadmap: } ( 6... c5 7. Nf3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 { — Maroczy Bind: against 6... c5, we skip sharp 7. d5 Benoni theory and play 7. Nf3 cxd4 8. Nxd4, steering into a clean positional Maroczy grip.<br><br>What if Black tries 6... Nc6?: } ) ( 6... Nc6 7. d5 Ne5 8. f4 { — Central Clamp: 6... Nc6 walks straight into 7. d5! Ne5 8. f4!, driving the knight back to d7 before White expands with g4 and a4.<br><br>Against flexible 6... Nbd7?: } ) ( 6... Nbd7 7. Nf3 e5 8. d5 { — Hybrid Setup: 7. Nf3! sidesteps premature 7. g4 c5! and scores 68.5% online, meeting 7... e5 with 8. d5 Nc5 9. Nd2.<br><br>And if Black starts with 6... Na6?: } ) ( 6... Na6 7. g4 c5 8. Nge2 { — Rim Knight Test: 7. g4! clamps f5 immediately — 7... e5 8. d5 transposes to our mainline, while 7... c5 meets 8. Nge2 cxd4 9. Nxd4. } ) 7. d5 { 7. d5! is the main closed tabiya, scoring 63.3% in Masters and +0.74 in Stockfish 19. Across Chapter 4 you will master four repeatable plans:<br><br>• Karpov g4 + Nge2-g3 Clamp — stopping ...f5 cold before pushing h4-h5 and opening the kingside.<br><br>• Queenside O-O-O + b4 Clamp When Black Skips ...a5 — if Black opens the c-file via ...c6 and ...cxd5 with the a-pawn still back on a7 or a6, castle O-O-O + Kb1 and meet ...b5 with b4! as in Salem vs. Amin; against ...a5, keep b4 shut and play for h4-h5 or Nc1-b3.<br><br>• Maroczy cxd4 Nxd4 Grip — centralizing with Be2, O-O, and Bxd4 to suffocate Black's dark squares against ...c5.<br><br>• Nc6-d5 + f4 Space Wedge — kicking Black's knights backward and seizing both central and flank territory. } { [%csl Gd5,Ge4] [%cal Gg2g4,Bg1e2] } *

