{ This is the flagship tabiya of the Makagonov / Karpov System. After 6... e5 7. d5 Na6, we unleash 8. g4! before Black can dream of ...f5, cement e4 with 9. f3, connect our rooks via 10. Qd2, and bring the knight to e2-g3 for +0.60+. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 { 4... d6 is the main KID move order at 77% online and 91% in Masters — 4... O-O is covered in Line 3.5. Notice how we punish immediate 4... c5 before ...d6: } ( 4... c5 5. dxc5 Qa5 6. Bd3 { — Rare 4... c5 skips ...d6, so 5. dxc5! grabs a central pawn and meets 5... Qa5 with calm 6. Bd3 O-O 7. Ne2 Qxc5 8. Be3 at +0.85. } ) 5. h3 $1 { 5. h3! prevents future ...Bg4 sorties before they start and avoids committing our kingside knight too early. Why not autopilot 5. Nf3 or 5. Be2? Both allow Black easy ...Bg4 or ...Na6-c5 setups, whereas 5. h3 prepares Be3 and g4 with maximum flexibility. } { [%csl Gh3,Yg4] [%cal Bc1e3,Gg2g4] } 5... O-O { 5... O-O is played nearly 90% of the time, tucking Black's king away before challenging the center. See how White handles Black's three early move-5 alternatives — 5... c6, 5... Nc6, and 5... c5: } ( 5... c6 6. Be3 { — 5... c6 prepares ...a6/...b5 or ...e5; 6. Be3 develops naturally and keeps +0.62, transposing to Line 3.4 once Black castles.<br><br>What if Black tries 5... Nc6?: } ) ( 5... Nc6 6. d5 { — 5... Nc6 allows immediate 6. d5! with tempo on the knight — stronger than 6. Be3, gaining space at +0.78 before ...Ne5 meets f4.<br><br>And if Black strikes with 5... c5?: } ) ( 5... c5 6. d5 { — Against early 5... c5, 6. d5! locks in a favorable Benoni with h3 already preventing ...Bg4, +0.66. } ) 6. Be3 { 6. Be3 develops with central support on d4 and prepares Qd2 + O-O-O batteries whenever needed. } { [%csl Ye3] [%cal Be3d4,Gd4d5] } 6... e5 { 6... e5 strikes at the center immediately — Black's most common reply. Rare 6... b6 gives us a free hand in the center: } ( 6... b6 7. Bd3 { — Against slow 6... b6, 7. Bd3 claims strong central control and prepares Nge2 or Nf3 with +0.60+. } ) 7. d5 $1 { 7. d5! is the heart of the Karpov System, played 97% online and 99% in Masters at +0.66. Why never autopilot 7. Nf3 or 7. dxe5 here? 7. Nf3 allows ...Nc6 or ...exd4 leaving White with little edge around +0.19 to +0.20, while 7. dxe5 dxe5 8. Nf3 Nc6 releases central tension into flat +0.00 equality.<br><br>Locking with 7. d5 shuts down Black's g7-bishop, fixes the c4-d5-e4 chain, and sets up immediate queenside space plus our g4 clamp. } { [%csl Gd5,Re5] [%cal Gg2g4,Bg1e2] } 7... Na6 { 7... Na6 is Black's top Masters choice at 53%, eyeing the c5-square. Check two passive alternatives — 7... c5 and 7... Ne8 — that hand us easy kingside expansion: } ( 7... c5 8. g4 { — 7... c5 locks the queenside in White's favor — 8. g4! immediately seizes kingside space with a massive +1.70 edge!<br><br>What about 7... Ne8?: } ) ( 7... Ne8 8. g4 { — Retreating 7... Ne8 signals ...f5 too transparently; 8. g4! stops ...f5 cold and puts White at +1.12. } ) 8. g4 $1 { 8. g4! is Karpov's signature thrust, scoring 63.5% in Masters and +0.66 to +0.77 in Stockfish 19. By seizing g4 immediately, we deny ...f5, take away h5 from Black's pieces, and clear the g-file and g3-square for a future kingside attack. } { [%csl Gg4,Rf5] [%cal Gg4f5,Bg1e2] } 8... Nc5 { 8... Nc5 lands on c5 with tempo against e4 — played 89% in Masters and 100% online. If Black inserts 8... c6 instead: } ( 8... c6 9. f3 { — 8... c6 leaves the a6-knight passive; 9. f3 cements e4 and prepares Qd2 + h4 at +0.82. } ) 9. f3 $1 { 9. f3! is the cleanest anchor, scoring 64.5% in Masters and 62.1% online. It permanently defends e4, blunts Black's c5-knight, and frees our queen to step to d2 while keeping our kingside pawns rock solid. } { [%csl Gf3,Ge4] [%cal Bd1d2,Be1c1] } 9... a5 { 9... a5 secures the c5-knight against b2-b4 — Black plays this 77% in Masters and 100% online. Watch how White answers immediate 9... c6 or premature 9... h5: } ( 9... c6 10. h4 { — Because Black omitted ...a5, 10. h4! launches a direct h4-h5 kingside battering ram at +1.00.<br><br>What if Black challenges g4 with 9... h5?: } ) ( 9... h5 10. Qd2 { — 9... h5?! weakens g5 prematurely; 10. Qd2 develops calmly and keeps +0.96, ready for O-O-O or Rh2. } ) 10. Qd2 { 10. Qd2 connects our back rank, forms a Q+B battery along the c1-h6 diagonal, and prepares either O-O-O or Nge2-g3 depending on Black's pawn break. } { [%csl Yd2,Ye3] [%cal Be3h6,Be1c1] } 10... c6 { 10... c6 prepares ...cxd5 to fight for the c-file before we castle queenside. Passive 10... Ne8 gets punished cleanly: } ( 10... Ne8 11. O-O-O { — 10... Ne8 retreats without challenging the center; 11. O-O-O! secures our king and prepares h4-h5 and g5 at +1.51. } ) 11. Nge2 { 11. Nge2 keeps our king's position flexible, prepares Ng3 to reinforce f5 and h5, and leaves both O-O-O and queenside expansion open.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.59 to +0.83 — Clear Space Edge in the Main Karpov Clamp<br><br>• Our Plan — Reroute Ne2-g3, push h4-h5 when Black plays ...cxd5 or ...h5, and either lock Black's g7-bishop in with h5-h6 or open the h-file via hxg6. Against ...a4, press the kingside pawnstorm or answer 11... h5 with 12. Nc1 and Nb3; reserve O-O-O + Kb1 + b4! strictly for move orders where Black omitted ...a5.<br><br>• Watch Out — Against 11... a4, 12. h4! invites a tactical piece sacrifice on g4 and e4 that backfires when Qb4 attacks b7, a4, and d6. Also watch ...cxd5 cxd5 followed by ...Bd7 and ...Rc8 — answer 11... h5 with 12. Nc1 and Nb3 to neutralize the c5-knight.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Keep our dark-squared e3-bishop unless Bxc5 damages Black's structure, and trade the c5-knight via Nc1-b3 only when it lands our pieces on active dark squares. Note that Salem vs. Amin's O-O-O + b4 clamp applies specifically when Black leaves the pawn on a6 rather than ...a5.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Click any continuation below to see White's h4-sacrifice refutation, c-file attack, and Nc1-b3 maneuver: } { [%csl Be2,Gd5] [%cal Be2g3,Gh3h4,Be3h6] } ( 11. Nge2 a4 12. h4 Bxg4 13. fxg4 Ncxe4 14. Nxe4 Nxe4 15. Qb4 { — Tactical check: 12. h4! allows 12... Bxg4?! 13. fxg4 Ncxe4 14. Nxe4 Nxe4 15. Qb4, where White's extra piece and b7/d6 counter-threats hold +0.62. } ) ( 11. Nge2 cxd5 12. cxd5 a4 13. Ng3 h5 14. h4 { — If Black opens the c-file first, 12. cxd5 a4 13. Ng3 h5 14. h4 locks down f5 and opens kingside attacking avenues at +0.83. } ) ( 11. Nge2 h5 12. Nc1 cxd5 13. cxd5 Bd7 14. Nb3 { — Against early 11... h5, 12. Nc1! reroutes via b3 to challenge Black's prized c5-knight while keeping +1.06. } ) *

