{ In 2000-2500 online play, 7... a5 is Black's #1 reply at 37.6% — securing c5 before moving the b8-knight. We answer with 8. g4 Na6 9. Nge2! Nc5 10. Ng3! c6 11. Qd2, omitting f3 so our kingside knight immediately dominates f5 and h5. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. h3 O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. d5 a5 { 7... a5 stakes out the c5-square immediately before developing the b8-knight, hoping to deter b4 and prepare ...Nc5 or ...a4. } { [%csl Ra5,Yc5] [%cal Rb8a6] } 8. g4 { 8. g4! stops ...f5 on the spot and scores 66.8% in Masters and 59.7% online at +0.73. } { [%csl Gg4,Rf5] [%cal Gg4f5,Bg1e2] } 8... Na6 { 8... Na6 heads straight for c5 — played 93-96% of the time. If Black tries 8... Nfd7 instead: } ( 8... Nfd7 9. Nge2 { — 8... Nfd7 leaves a6 and c5 uncoordinated; 9. Nge2 develops smoothly toward g3 with +0.98. } ) 9. Nge2 $1 { 9. Nge2! is Stockfish's #1 move, +0.64 to +0.84, and beats autopilot 9. Nf3 at +0.56. Why? Because from e2 the knight leaps straight to g3 — guarding e4 without needing f3 right away and aiming directly at f5 and h5. } { [%csl Ye2,Yf5] [%cal Be2g3,Gg4g5] } 9... Nc5 { 9... Nc5 is played 70% online; 9... Nd7 and 9... c6 are covered in Lines 1.3 and 1.4. } { [%csl Rc5] [%cal Be2g3] } 10. Ng3 { 10. Ng3! is 100% unanimous in both Masters and 2000-2500 online games! The knight defends e4 cleanly while freeing Be2, Qd2, and h4-h5. } { [%csl Bg3,Rf5] [%cal Bg3f5,Gh3h4] } 10... c6 { 10... c6 prepares ...cxd5 and ...a4 counterplay. Watch how sharply White punishes slow 10... Bd7, played 40% in Masters: } ( 10... Bd7 11. Qd2 { — 10... Bd7 walks straight into 11. Qd2! at +1.37 to +1.48 — threatening g5 and h4-h5 with overwhelming kingside pressure. } ) 11. Qd2 { 11. Qd2 connects our rooks, builds the e3-d2 battery along the c1-h6 diagonal, and keeps both O-O-O and Be2+h4 ready for +0.77 to +0.97.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.77 to +0.97 — Active Karpov Clamp With h4-h5-h6 Pawnstorm<br><br>• Our Plan — Two flagship kingside plans work here: push f3 + h4-h5-h6! to entomb Black's g7-bishop permanently on h8 — the same kingside lock seen after 9. Nf3 in Duda vs. Korobov — or crack open the h-file with g5 + h4-h5-hxg6 followed by Bh3-Rh4-Nd5 as in Ding Liren vs. Amonatov. If Black holds the queenside, tuck O-O-O + Kb1 and deploy Be2 + Rc1.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black wants 11... a4 to clamp b2 or 11... cxd5 followed by ...Qa5 and ...b5; if O-O-O feels sharp against ...b5, White can also keep the king on e1/f1-g2 while storming with h4-h5. Keep c3 defended against ...Qa5.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Keep our g3-knight aimed at f5 and avoid premature Bxc5; if Black plays ...cxd5, recapture cxd5 when launching f3 + h4-h5, or exd5 with O-O-O and Be2 to target d6 and e5.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Click any continuation below to see the O-O-O setup, the h4-h5 pawnstorm, and the punishment for slow 11... Ne8: } { [%csl Yd2,Bg3] [%cal Be3h6,Be1c1,Gh3h4] } ( 11. Qd2 a4 12. O-O-O cxd5 13. exd5 Bd7 14. Be2 { — SF19 #1: 12. O-O-O tucks the king safely away before 13. exd5 Bd7 14. Be2 eyes the kingside and d6 at +0.82. } ) ( 11. Qd2 cxd5 12. cxd5 a4 13. f3 Bd7 14. h4 Qa5 15. h5 { — Karpov Pawnstorm: 13. f3 locks e4 before 14. h4 and 15. h5! threaten h5-h6 to bury Bg7 or hxg6 to rip open the h-file at +0.97! } ) ( 11. Qd2 Ne8 12. O-O-O a4 13. f3 Nc7 14. h4 { — Slow 11... Ne8 lets White castle queenside effortlessly and roll 13. f3 + 14. h4-h5 at +1.43! } ) *

