{ When Black chooses 9... Nd7, 30% online, 25% Masters, instead of 9... Nc5, they want to maneuver the knight to c5 or play ...Bf6-h4 to harass g3. Skipping 10. Ng3 in favor of 10. Qd2! stops ...Bf6-h4 cold and lands Ng3 with +0.99 to +1.10! } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. h3 O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. d5 a5 8. g4 Na6 9. Nge2 Nd7 { 9... Nd7 prepares ...Nc5 to keep the a6-knight flexible for ...b6/...Nb4 or ...f5, while also opening the dark-squared bishop's path to f6-h4. } { [%csl Rd7,Ya6] } 10. Qd2 $1 { 10. Qd2! is Stockfish's #1 choice at +0.91 and stronger than autopilot 10. Ng3 at +0.54 to +0.87. Why wait a move to play Ng3? Because immediate Ng3 allows ...Bf6 to hit h4 right away, whereas 10. Qd2 covers h4 and prepares O-O-O! } { [%csl Yd2,Rh4] [%cal Be1c1,Be2g3] } 10... Ndc5 { 10... Ndc5 plants a knight on c5 while the a6-knight still lingers on the rim — played 100% online. } 11. Ng3 { 11. Ng3! now lands cleanly on g3 with h4 guarded by the queen, giving White a commanding +0.99 to +1.24 edge.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.99 to +1.24 — Large Space Advantage & Central X-Ray Pressure<br><br>• Our Plan — Against early ...c6, answer calmly with Be2! and Rd1!, X-raying Black's backward d6-pawn; once Black plays ...cxd5, recapture Nxd5! instead of cxd5, threatening Nb6 and Qxd6 while keeping c4 intact. If Black keeps tension closed, tuck O-O-O, meet ...a4 with f3 and Kb1, then march h4-h5-h6! to bury Bg7 on h8 or prise open the h-file.<br><br>• Watch Out — Meet 11... Nb4 with quiet 12. Be2! c6 13. h4, refusing to trade off your dark-squared bishop; leaving Black's a6-knight stranded on the rim hands White a winning edge in every transition.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Keep both bishops unless capturing on c5 or d5 directly wins material or shatters Black's pawns; leaving Black with an offside a6-knight favors White in all queenside and central endgames.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Click any continuation below to see White's Rd1 + Nxd5 d-file hammer across Black's three main replies: } { [%csl Bg3,Yd2] [%cal Be1c1,Gh3h4,Bg3f5] } ( 11. Ng3 a4 12. Be2 c6 13. Rd1 cxd5 14. Nxd5 Ne6 15. Nb6 Rb8 16. Qxd6 { — SF19 #1 at depth 20, +1.09: 12. Be2 and 13. Rd1! answer ...cxd5 with 14. Nxd5!, followed by 15. Nb6 Rb8 16. Qxd6 winning the backward d6-pawn cleanly. } ) ( 11. Ng3 c6 12. Rd1 a4 13. Be2 cxd5 14. Nxd5 Ne6 15. Nb6 Rb8 16. Qxd6 { — SF19 #2 at +1.12: immediate 11... c6 runs into the exact same 12. Rd1! a4 13. Be2 cxd5 14. Nxd5 Ne6 15. Nb6 Rb8 16. Qxd6 collapse. } ) ( 11. Ng3 Bd7 12. Be2 c6 13. Rd1 cxd5 14. Nxd5 Ne6 15. Nb6 Rb8 16. Qxd6 { — SF19 #3 at +1.24: even with 11... Bd7 developing first, 12. Be2 c6 13. Rd1! cxd5 14. Nxd5! crushes d6 on the d-file. } ) *

