{ In Masters play, 9... c6 is Black's #1 choice, 28% — trying to open the c-file against c3 and c2 before Black commits the a6-knight. We answer 10. Ng3 cxd5 with 11. cxd5!, keeping our e4-anchor intact and claiming the open c-file. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. h3 O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. d5 a5 8. g4 Na6 9. Nge2 c6 { 9... c6 attacks d5 right away. If Black lashes out on the flank with 9... h5 instead: } ( 9... h5 10. Bg5 { — 9... h5 weakens g5 prematurely; 10. Bg5! pins the f6-knight to Black's queen at +0.64 and prepares Qd2. } ) 10. Ng3 { 10. Ng3 develops calmly toward f5 and h5 while keeping f3 flexible. } { [%csl Bg3,Ge4] [%cal Bg3f5,Bf1e2] } 10... cxd5 { 10... cxd5 opens the c-file immediately, hoping to create counterplay against c2 and b2. } 11. cxd5 $1 { 11. cxd5! is vital — scoring +0.64 versus just +0.25 for autopilot 11. exd5. Why capture with the c-pawn? Keeping our pawn on e4 prevents ...e4 breaks, preserves our spatial chain, opens the c-file for Rc1, and leaves Black's a6-knight biting on granite.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.67 to +0.93 — Clean Space Edge With Kingside Storm & c-File<br><br>• Our Plan — Play Be2 and h4! followed by g5 and h5-h6! or hxg6 — as in Cheparinov vs. Mamedov, where h4-g5-h5-hxg6 + gxh7+ cracks the king open. If Black plays ...Nd7-c5 to restrain g5, add f3 and Qd2, or push b4! once Black commits ...Rc8.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black tries ...Nc5 followed by ...h5 to target g4; if 11... Nc5 12. f3 h5 13. gxh5 Nxh5 14. Nxh5 Qh4+, step 15. Kd2! cleanly to keep the extra edge without exposing the king.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Keep our dark-squared bishop to challenge ...Bg7 or trade rooks only on favorable squares; Black's d6-pawn remains backward in endgames.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Click any continuation below to see the Be2 + h4-g5 pawnstorm, f3 + Be2 clamp, and tactical Kd2 defense: } { [%csl Gd5,Yb5] [%cal Bd1d2,Bf1e2,Ra1c1] } ( 11. cxd5 Bd7 12. Be2 Nc5 13. h4 a4 14. g5 { — SF19 #1: 12. Be2 Nc5 13. h4 a4 14. g5 launches the classic h5-h6 / hxg6 pawnstorm at +0.67. } ) ( 11. cxd5 Nd7 12. h4 Ndc5 13. f3 Bf6 14. g5 { — Against 11... Nd7, 12. h4! and 14. g5 drive Black's bishop back and dominate the kingside at +0.90. } ) ( 11. cxd5 Nc5 12. f3 h5 13. gxh5 Nxh5 14. Nxh5 Qh4+ 15. Kd2 { — If 11... Nc5 12. f3 h5 13. gxh5 Nxh5 14. Nxh5 Qh4+, 15. Kd2! keeps the king safe before Bxc5! dxc5 wrecks Black's structure at +0.93. } ) *

