king Black's pawns into weak c5/e5 targets while White consolidates with Kb1 or d-file rooks after castling. If Black challenges early with 10... c6, switch immediately to the opposite wing with 11. a3! and b4!, ripping open the a-file.<br><br>• Watch Out — Meet 10... Ne8 11. Qc2 f5 with O-O-O + a3, or as in Niemann vs. Amin play g4 f5 g5 followed by exf5 gxf5, f3, and Nde4! If kingside pawns lock up, never miss h4-h5-h6! to bury Black's g7-bishop on h8.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — White's central e4-square, d5 space, and sound pawn majority dominate once ...f5 or ...c6 fixes Black's c5/e5 pawn shell.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Click any continuation below to see White's timed Bxc5 trade, O-O squeeze, and a3 + b4 flank punch: } { [%csl Ye2,Gd5] [%cal Gg2g4,Be2g4,Ra1c1] } ( 10. Be2 Ne8 11. Qc2 f5 12. O-O-O Nf6 13. a3 Qe7 14. Bxc5 dxc5 15. Kb1 { — SF19 #1 at depth 20, +0.57: only after Black commits with ...f5 and ...Qe7 does 14. Bxc5! fix c5 and hand White an easy plan. } ) ( 10. Be2 Bd7 11. Qc2 c6 12. O-O Qe7 13. Rfd1 Rfc8 14. Bxc5 dxc5 15. dxc6 { — SF19 #2 at +0.58: against 10... Bd7 and ...c6, 12. O-O and 14. Bxc5 followed by 15. dxc6 isolate Black's c5-pawn. } ) ( 10. Be2 c6 11. a3 Nfd7 12. b4 axb4 13. axb4 Rxa1 14. Qxa1 Na6 15. Qa3 { — SF19 #3 at +0.94: immediate 10... c6 runs straight into 11. a3! and 12. b4!, seizing the open a-file. } ) *

